====================================================================================================
D. Poynting's theorem for arbitrary fields that satisfy Maxwell's equations (eps(r), mu(r) time-invariant)
====================================================================================================
   sample terms (dw/dt, div S, J.E): (-4.444, +0.659, +3.785), (-6.547, +0.897, +5.650), (-3.269, +0.277, +2.992)
PASS  Faraday holds identically for E = -grad Phi - dA/dt, B = curl A (residual of curl E + dB/dt)   [5.9e-13]
PASS  J.E term is exercised (J from Ampere-Maxwell is not zero)
PASS  dw/dt + div S + J.E = 0 at 5 random (r, t), eps and mu varying in space (point form, page section 2)   [max rel residual 1.3e-11]
PASS  negative control: with eps(t) the residual is -1/2 (d eps/dt) E^2, so 'eps, mu constant in time' is needed   [residual -0.3030, predicted -0.3030]
PASS  E.dD/dt = d/dt(1/2 eps E.E) when eps does not depend on t

====================================================================================================
E. The one-dimensional check (Rao's route) in the folded derivation
====================================================================================================
PASS  dEx/dz = -mu dHy/dt for the planar fields (component Faraday)
PASS  dSz/dz = -mu Hy dHy/dt - Ex (Jx + eps dEx/dt)   (middle line of the 1D derivation)
PASS  dSz/dz = -d/dt(1/2 mu Hy^2 + 1/2 eps Ex^2) - Jx Ex   (last line)
PASS  curl(x Ex(z,t)) = y dEx/dz and curl(y Hy(z,t)) = -x dHy/dz (so the two component equations are right)

